   "text": {
    "L1": "Numbers can hide patterns in their digits. We can test those patterns instead of guessing.",
    "L2": "A digit sum adds the digits, so 4752 has digit sum 18.",
    "L3": "A bigger number does not always have a bigger digit sum: 99 has sum 18, but 1000 has sum 1.",
    "L4": "A palindrome reads the same forwards and backwards, like 121.",
    "L5": "With reverse-and-add, 47 plus its reverse, 74, makes 121, a palindrome.",
    "L6": "For the Kaprekar routine, arrange four digits largest-first and smallest-first, keeping every zero, then subtract.",
    "L7": "Starting with 2111, the routine reaches 6174, and 6174 repeats when the routine is continued.",
    "L8": "But a few successful examples do not prove a pattern for every number. A conjecture needs full checking or an explanation."
   },
